-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+Acceptance assigns a revision ID, monotonic per visit.
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+## Conflict Detection and Visit State
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+A submission whose base revision ID matches the visit's current revision ID replaces it as the current record. A submission whose base revision ID is anything else — including an edit started before another edit landed — is stored as an **unresolved candidate** and leaves the current record unchanged. A base revision ID naming a revision that does not exist for the visit is treated like any other non-current base. A visit with one or more unresolved candidates is **unresolved**.
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+While a visit is unresolved, every further submission for that visit is held as a candidate, even one based on the current record. Holding these submissions keeps the coordinator's comparison set complete instead of letting the current record advance past evidence the review has not seen.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+While unresolved, current-state retrieval returns the current record, the unresolved flag, and the candidates. The current record changes only through coordinator resolution, never through arrival of new submissions.
 
-## Downstream Contract Work
+## Resolution
 
-After selection, define the submission envelope, current-state response, unresolved-work representation, and resolution operation here. Tablet-sync will size its local storage and retry state machine around that envelope. Review-export will size its coordinator interface and export eligibility rules around the unresolved-work representation. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
+A coordinator resolves an unresolved visit by adopting one candidate or by affirming the current record. Adoption makes the candidate the current record and retains the previously current record as a superseded revision. Affirmation marks every competing candidate superseded. Superseded revisions stay queryable in the submission history; nothing is deleted.
+
+The coordinator does not edit visit fields as part of resolution. If neither the current record nor any candidate is right, the coordinator submits a corrected visit as a new submission, authored by them and based on the revision they are correcting — the same path a field worker uses. This keeps authorship honest and the audit path uniform.
+
+Every resolution records the coordinator's identity, the outcome, and the revisions involved.
+
+## Downstream Contract Notes
+
+Tablet-sync sizes its local storage and retry state machine around the submission envelope, and shows field workers which of their submissions are current, held as candidates, or superseded. Review-export sizes its coordinator queue around the unresolved-work representation and excludes unresolved visits from the export set. Both link back to this node's specs rather than inventing their own concurrency semantics.
+
+## Decisions & Alternatives
+
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|---|---|---|---|
+| Concurrency policy for competing edits | Whole-visit revisions sent to review | Field-aware three-way merge combining disjoint field edits automatically | Preserves each worker's submitted visit as a scientific unit; avoids maintained merge rules that could silently combine scientifically related edits, such as a species identification and its cover estimate. Costs a larger review queue, which the team accepted. |
+| Submissions while a visit is unresolved | Held as candidates, even when based on the current record | Apply immediately when based on the current record | Keeps the review's comparison set stable and the path from submission to export inspectable; prevents the current record from advancing past material the review has not seen. |
+| Coordinator resolution | Selection among submitted revisions; corrections are new coordinator-authored submissions | Free-form coordinator editing of the resolved record | Keeps every field value traceable to an authored submission; avoids a second, privileged write path into worker records. |
+
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
+
+### Resolved
+
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy: whole-visit revisions (see Decisions & Alternatives).
+
+### Deferred
+
+1. Whether new edits may reference archived plots — the site catalog retains archived plot UUIDs for historical records, but whether this node should accept new submissions against them is open pending tablet-sync and review-export design.
+2. Candidate accumulation — no cap on unresolved candidates per visit is set for the first release; revisit if review queues grow unboundedly.
+
